A busy morning brings hundreds of reports about the same thing. We keep one ticket per issue
and merge every duplicate into it, so engineering sees one thread with a count.

## The rule

A report is a duplicate when evidence establishes the **same failure mechanism**, not merely the same subject. This is proposed replacement guidance under [decision 0002](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause); [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) is preserved as history:

1. Read the body and identify the product, tenant, channel, and booking-form version.
2. Look up tenant locale and reproduce expected versus actual behavior on that input path.
3. Compare the demonstrated cause with an existing ticket. Merge only when it agrees; otherwise open a separate ticket or hold the report for investigation. Deduplicate export rows by report ID.

<Callout>
Subjects and intake tags help find candidates. They do not establish a shared cause. See the [#4471 review](/kb/incidents/4471-review) for the demonstrated failure of subject-only merging.
</Callout>

## Try it

<DedupeExplorer />

## When you are unsure

Do not merge an unknown tenant or an unexplained one-day shift by guesswork. Record the uncertainty and request the missing context. Read the body even when the automated classifier returns a known cause.
